= Knowledge ecosystem =

A '''knowledge ecosystem''' is a network or system of interdependent people, organizations, communities, technologies, institutions, and knowledge resources whose interactions support the creation, search, validation, exchange, recombination, preservation, and use of [[Knowledge]]. The concept emphasizes distributed relationships and evolving interdependence rather than treating knowledge as a stock of documents controlled by a single organization.

Contemporary ecosystem research commonly distinguishes knowledge ecosystems from business, innovation, and entrepreneurial ecosystems by their organizing purpose. One influential formulation describes knowledge ecosystems as users and producers of knowledge organized around a '''joint knowledge search'''.<ref name="jarvi">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.respol.2018.05.007 Järvi, K., Almpanopoulou, A., and Ritala, P. (2018), “Organization of knowledge ecosystems: Prefigurative and partial forms,” ''Research Policy'' 47(8), 1523–1537.]</ref> Actual systems can combine several ecosystem types, so the distinction is analytical rather than a claim that every real network belongs to only one category.

== Why use an ecosystem perspective? ==

Traditional [[Knowledge management]] often focuses on how one organization captures, organizes, shares, and reuses knowledge. An ecosystem perspective becomes useful when important knowledge processes cross organizational boundaries.

Examples include:

* universities, laboratories, funders, journals, repositories, and firms participating in scientific research;
* open-source communities coordinating developers, users, maintainers, documentation, and infrastructure;
* regional innovation networks linking universities, firms, public agencies, and professional communities;
* healthcare networks in which research evidence, clinical practice, patient experience, and regulation interact.

No single actor controls the whole system. Participants can cooperate in some activities while competing in others.

== Components ==

A knowledge ecosystem can be analyzed through several interacting components.

=== Actors ===

Actors may include individuals, teams, organizations, communities, governments, universities, firms, publishers, platforms, professional societies, and artificial computational systems.

Actors differ in expertise, resources, authority, incentives, and access. Treating them as interchangeable graph nodes can hide these asymmetries.

=== Knowledge resources ===

Resources include publications, datasets, software, procedures, standards, models, tacit expertise, experimental methods, repositories, vocabularies, and institutional memory.

A stored resource is not automatically reliable knowledge. Its provenance, evidence, version, interpretation, and maintenance status matter.

=== Infrastructure ===

Digital repositories, communication systems, search engines, identity services, metadata standards, APIs, laboratories, libraries, and collaboration platforms enable knowledge flows.

Infrastructure shapes what can be discovered and reused. Search ranking, access controls, file formats, identifiers, and interoperability can influence which knowledge becomes visible.

=== Institutions and governance ===

Rules determine:

* who may contribute;
* which claims are reviewed or accepted;
* how disputes are resolved;
* what evidence is required;
* how credit and responsibility are assigned;
* which resources are public or restricted;
* how errors are corrected;
* how standards evolve.

Governance is therefore part of the knowledge system rather than an external administrative detail.

== Core processes ==

Knowledge ecosystems can support several recurring processes.

'''Creation''' produces new claims, methods, models, artifacts, or skills.

'''Search and discovery''' connect questions to relevant resources or expertise.

'''Exchange and transfer''' move representations or practices among actors.

'''Recombination''' integrates previously separate knowledge.

'''Validation''' tests claims against evidence, methods, standards, or peer criticism.

'''Retention''' preserves knowledge through documents, software, institutions, and trained participants.

'''Revision and correction''' update or reject material when evidence changes.

'''Application''' connects knowledge to decisions, design, policy, production, or other action.

These processes can form feedback loops. Application can reveal errors that trigger new research; new methods can change what data are collected; standards can alter the kinds of contributions that enter the system.

== Openness and boundaries ==

A knowledge ecosystem needs some boundary for analysis, but knowledge flows frequently cross that boundary.

An ecosystem may be open to external contributors, publications, datasets, or standards while maintaining internal review and coordination. Openness can increase access to diverse expertise, but it can also increase verification and coordination costs.

A closed ecosystem can protect confidential knowledge or maintain strong standards, but excessive closure can reduce diversity and create lock-in.

The relevant question is therefore not whether openness is always good, but which flows should be open, under what conditions, with what provenance and governance.

== Network structure ==

Knowledge ecosystems are often modeled as networks. Actors or resources can be nodes, while collaboration, citation, contribution, funding, mentorship, access, reuse, or communication can form different edge types.

Network structure affects diffusion and coordination. Highly central actors can accelerate dissemination but also create dependency or gatekeeping. Dense communities can support trust and shared language while also increasing correlated beliefs or exclusion.

Connectivity is therefore not equivalent to epistemic quality.

A network can efficiently circulate an error.

== Knowledge development ==

Empirical research on knowledge ecosystems emphasizes organization and interaction rather than simple accumulation. A study of a university integrated into a regional strategic network identified reinforcing mechanisms of structure and openness in knowledge development.<ref name="brink">[https://doi.org/10.1108/JKM-11-2021-0814 “Mechanisms of knowledge development in a knowledge ecosystem,” ''Journal of Knowledge Management'' 26(11), 2022.]</ref>

Such findings are context-dependent. They identify mechanisms observed in particular systems rather than universal laws of every knowledge ecosystem.

== Creation and organizational knowledge ==

Knowledge-creation research such as Nonaka's organizational theory distinguishes interactions among tacit and explicit forms of knowledge and emphasizes dynamic social processes rather than simple document storage.<ref name="nonaka">[https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.5.1.14 Nonaka, I. (1994), “A Dynamic Theory of Organizational Knowledge Creation,” ''Organization Science'' 5(1), 14–37.]</ref>

Knowledge-ecosystem analysis extends the scale beyond one organization. Tacit expertise can move through collaboration, personnel mobility, apprenticeship, or shared practice even when it cannot be fully encoded in a repository.

== Knowledge ecosystems and innovation ecosystems ==

The terms '''knowledge ecosystem''' and '''innovation ecosystem''' overlap but are not synonymous.

A knowledge ecosystem is organized analytically around knowledge production, search, and circulation. An innovation ecosystem is usually organized around complementary actors jointly producing or enabling innovations or a system-level value proposition.

A systematic review comparing ecosystem types found meaningful differences in boundaries and goals among business, innovation, entrepreneurial, and knowledge ecosystems.<ref name="cobben">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbusres.2021.12.046 Cobben, D. et al. (2022), “Ecosystem types: A systematic review on boundaries and goals,” ''Journal of Business Research'' 142, 138–164.]</ref>

One real network can simultaneously participate in knowledge creation and commercial innovation.

== Incentives and power ==

Knowledge flows are shaped by incentives.

Academic researchers may be rewarded for publications; firms may protect proprietary knowledge; open-source maintainers may value reputation or shared infrastructure; governments may require public access; platforms may optimize engagement.

These incentives can conflict.

Power also matters. Actors controlling funding, publication, infrastructure, standards, or access can shape which questions are pursued and which contributions are visible. An ecosystem account that treats all participants as equal can obscure structural exclusion.

== Epistemic quality ==

A knowledge ecosystem should not be evaluated only by volume, speed, or connectivity.

Relevant quality indicators can include:

* provenance completeness;
* reproducibility;
* independent verification;
* citation and reuse with context;
* diversity of independent evidence;
* correction latency;
* uncertainty reporting;
* robustness to conflicting evidence;
* access and participation disparities;
* retention of negative or null results.

A densely connected ecosystem with fast dissemination can still produce groupthink or coordinated misinformation.

== Scholarly knowledge ecosystems ==

Science provides a prominent example. Research involves laboratories, universities, journals, preprint servers, repositories, funders, professional societies, databases, software, peer review, and policy institutions.

A review of the scholarly knowledge ecosystem highlights challenges requiring multi-sector coordination, mixed methods, and interdisciplinary analysis.<ref name="scholarly">[https://doi.org/10.3389/frma.2021.751553 “The Scholarly Knowledge Ecosystem: Challenges and Opportunities for the Field of Information,” ''Frontiers in Research Metrics and Analytics'' 6, 2021.]</ref>

Open science changes this ecosystem through public repositories, persistent identifiers, open-source software, preprints, data sharing, and new review mechanisms, while also creating new governance and sustainability problems.

== Digital and artificial knowledge agents ==

Search engines, recommender systems, knowledge graphs, language models, automated theorem provers, and other computational systems can participate in knowledge workflows.

Their roles should be described operationally. A retrieval system can improve discovery; a model can generate candidate explanations; an automated checker can validate formal constraints.

These functions do not automatically make an artificial system a reliable knower. Generated claims still require provenance, evaluation, error detection, and accountability.

Artificial agents can also increase correlated errors when many actors rely on the same model or training data.

== Resilience and failure modes ==

Knowledge ecosystems can fail through:

* concentration of authority;
* loss of critical infrastructure;
* incompatible standards;
* poor provenance;
* inaccessible knowledge;
* incentives for low-quality output;
* misinformation cascades;
* homogeneous participant pools;
* lack of maintenance;
* dependence on a small number of experts;
* suppressed disagreement;
* excessive fragmentation.

Resilience depends on redundancy, recoverability, diversity, interoperability, governance, and mechanisms for correction.

== Measuring a knowledge ecosystem ==

No single scalar score captures ecosystem health. Evaluation should start from an explicit purpose.

Possible measures include:

* number and diversity of active contributors;
* cross-boundary collaboration;
* search success;
* time to locate expertise;
* reuse of data, software, or methods;
* independent replication;
* correction time;
* knowledge retention;
* access inequality;
* network concentration;
* outcome quality for specific tasks.

Metrics can create incentives, so their behavioral effects should also be monitored.

== Relation to dispersed knowledge ==

[[Dispersed knowledge]] emphasizes that relevant knowledge is distributed among people and places rather than centrally available. A knowledge ecosystem provides one systems framework for understanding how such distributed knowledge can be found, combined, challenged, and reused without assuming that it becomes fully centralized.

== Relation to information ecology ==

[[Information ecology]] examines relationships among information, people, practices, technologies, and environments. Knowledge ecosystems overlap strongly with that perspective but focus more explicitly on the production and use of knowledge and on multi-actor knowledge search.

